<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      **No warning survives a build.** The nullable analysis was on from the day the SDK
      template turned it on and nothing was annotated against it, so a build carried 662 of
      them - and a number that large is one nobody reads, which makes a new warning
      invisible. The annotations are now written, so this keeps them written: a warning
      the compiler raises stops the build rather than joining a list.

      One line is suppressed, at the line itself, with the reason on it - see
      `SheetGridReader.cs`. That is what an exception should look like: local, and next to
      what it is about.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      Server GC, because a conversion is one long allocation burst on a machine that has
      cores to spare. Workstation GC collects on the one thread doing the work, so every
      collection is time the conversion is stopped; server GC has a heap and a collector
      thread per core and does that work beside it.

      Measured on the sample project's full conversion, 20 logical cores: 71.3 s to 54.1 s.
      The usual objection to server GC is that it trades memory for time, and here it
      barely does - the peak is the live model rather than uncollected garbage, so it went
      from 10.28 GB to 10.63 GB. What would make this a real trade is a machine with fewer
      cores than heaps' worth of memory, which is why the number is written down: on a build
      agent with 2 cores and 4 GB there is nothing here to win and `DOTNET_gcServer=0`
      switches it off without a rebuild.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>false</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
    <!--
      Lower case, because this is what somebody types. The same `tabbit` on every
      platform, rather than a name whose capitals have to be remembered on the two where
      the shell cares about them.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>tabbit</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Tabbit</RootNamespace>

    <!--
      What the assembly says it is when something other than this repository asks - a
      file property sheet, a package listing, the copyright line the option parser
      prints. Written here rather than left to the SDK, which would answer with the
      assembly name and say nothing about what the program does.
    -->
    <Product>Tabbit</Product>
    <Description>Game Data Authoring &amp; Build Tool. Authors, validates, optimizes and builds static game data into TCB, a runtime binary a game reads without parsing.</Description>
    <Authors>Tabbit</Authors>

    <!--
      What a build with no version told to it reports. The release workflow overrides it
      with the tag, so the version option and the `toolVersion` recorded in every summary
      and history snapshot name the release a binary came from. A local build says 0.0.0
      rather than claiming to be 1.0.0, which is what the SDK default did.
    -->
    <Version>0.0.0</Version>

    <!--
      The executable's icon - what Explorer, the taskbar and a file property sheet show.
      Without it the program is the generic console icon, which says nothing about what
      the file is.

      Committed rather than generated at build time: `brand/build-assets.mjs` cuts it out
      of the source artwork, and requiring Node to build a .NET program would be a poor
      trade for one file that changes when the logo does. It carries 16 through 256 so
      Windows has a size to pick rather than resampling one.
    -->
    <ApplicationIcon>..\brand\dist\icons\tabbit.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!--
      Debug information goes inside the assembly rather than beside it, so a published
      directory is the program and nothing else.
    -->
    <DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

    <!--
      Stops the .pdb and .xml files that ship inside packages from being copied out.

      What made this urgent is gone: SkiaSharp arrived through NPOI and its debug file was
      83 MB, more than the rest of a self-contained publish put together, and neither
      package is a dependency any more. Kept because it costs nothing and the next package
      to carry one would otherwise put it in the publish unannounced.
    -->
    <AllowedReferenceRelatedFileExtensions>none</AllowedReferenceRelatedFileExtensions>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(BuildTimestamp)' != ''">
    <!--
      When this build was made, for the version option to print.

      Passed in rather than evaluated here, and nothing supplies it but the release
      workflow. `$([System.DateTime]::UtcNow)` written at this spot would be a compile
      input that differs on every build: deterministic builds would end, and every
      `dotnet build` would recompile from scratch - in front of a suite that already takes
      twenty-two minutes. A developer's build says no build time instead, which together
      with a version of 0.0.0 is the truthful answer that it is not a release.

      An `AssemblyMetadata` item is turned into an `AssemblyMetadataAttribute` by the SDK,
      so there is no generated file here to keep in step with anything.

      Deliberately not part of the informational version: that string is the build cache's
      key and the `toolVersion` recorded in every summary, and a value that moved on each
      build would mean a full conversion every time. spec/ops/cli-help.md section 7.
    -->
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="BuildTimestamp" Value="$(BuildTimestamp)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(SelfContained)' == 'true'">
    <!--
      A self-contained publish is one file that runs on a machine with no .NET at all.
      That is what makes the ASP.NET Core framework reference below free: the serve mode
      needs that runtime, and a build machine that only has the base one would otherwise
      be unable to run Tabbit for any purpose.
    -->
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <EnableCompressionInSingleFile>true</EnableCompressionInSingleFile>
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The Tcb readers, embedded so the generators can write them into their
      output. Each generated target is then self-contained: nothing to install, no
      include path to configure, and no chance of a consumer pairing generated code
      with a reader of a different vintage.

      One copy of each source, living under lib/ for anyone reading or editing it
      directly, so what ships cannot drift from what is committed.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\ts\tabbit\tcb_reader.ts"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Ts.tcb_reader.ts" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\ts\tabbit\updater.ts"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Ts.updater.ts" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\cs\tabbit\TcbReader.cs"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Cs.TcbReader.cs" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\cs\tabbit\TabbitUpdater.cs"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Cs.TabbitUpdater.cs" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\cpp\tabbit\tcb_reader.h"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Cpp.tcb_reader.h" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\cpp\tabbit\updater.h"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Cpp.updater.h" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\go\tabbit\tcb_reader.go"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Go.tcb_reader.go" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\go\tabbit\updater.go"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Go.updater.go" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\rust\tabbit\tcb_reader.rs"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Rust.tcb_reader.rs" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\rust\tabbit\updater.rs"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Rust.updater.rs" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\python\tabbit\tcb_reader.py"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Python.tcb_reader.py" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\python\tabbit\updater.py"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Python.updater.py" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\java\tabbit\TcbReader.java"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Java.TcbReader.java" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\java\tabbit\TabbitUpdater.java"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Java.TabbitUpdater.java" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\kotlin\tabbit\TcbReader.kt"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Kotlin.TcbReader.kt" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\kotlin\tabbit\TabbitUpdater.kt"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Kotlin.TabbitUpdater.kt" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\swift\tabbit\TcbReader.swift"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Swift.TcbReader.swift" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\swift\tabbit\Updater.swift"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Swift.Updater.swift" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\ruby\tabbit\tcb_reader.rb"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Ruby.tcb_reader.rb" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\ruby\tabbit\updater.rb"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Ruby.updater.rb" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\dart\tabbit\tcb_reader.dart"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Dart.tcb_reader.dart" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\dart\tabbit\updater.dart"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Dart.updater.dart" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\c\tabbit\tabbit_tcb_reader.h"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.C.tabbit_tcb_reader.h" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\c\tabbit\tabbit_updater.h"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.C.tabbit_updater.h" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\php\tabbit\TcbReader.php"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Php.TcbReader.php" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\php\tabbit\TabbitUpdater.php"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Php.TabbitUpdater.php" />

    <!--
      Lua ships four files: the reader, its two numeric backends - LuaJIT and 5.3+ spell
      low-level numeric work in mutually unparsable syntax - and the native C module's
      source, which is the encryption, MAC and manifest-hash path.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\lua\tabbit\tcb_reader.lua"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Lua.tcb_reader.lua" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\lua\tabbit\tcb_ops_jit.lua"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Lua.tcb_ops_jit.lua" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\lua\tabbit\tcb_ops_53.lua"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Lua.tcb_ops_53.lua" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\lua\tabbit\native\tabbit_native.c"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Lua.tabbit_native.c" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\lua\tabbit\updater.lua"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Lua.updater.lua" />

    <!--
      Unreal gets its own rather than the C++ one, because the engine already has every
      type that reader defines. Going through std::string and a Tabbit Uuid struct
      cost two allocations per string cell and a text parse per uuid, to build what
      FString and FGuid already are. And an Unreal module builds with exceptions off, so
      the throwing reader could not report a malformed file at all - the throw was a
      termination inside a function whose signature promised a bool.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\unreal\tabbit\TabbitTcbReader.h"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Unreal.TabbitTcbReader.h" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\unreal\tabbit\TabbitUpdater.h"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Unreal.TabbitUpdater.h" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\lib\unreal\tabbit\TabbitUpdater.cpp"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Runtime.Unreal.TabbitUpdater.cpp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Starting recipes the `template` option writes, embedded so that `tabbit` produces the
      same file wherever it was installed from.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="recipes\*.jsonc"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Recipes.%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The text of this tool's own reports, one file per owner and language: `core.en.json`
      is the core's English. A wildcard rather than a list, so that adding a language is
      adding a file and deleting a layout is deleting its file - there is nothing here to
      keep in step with either. The logical name is what MessageCatalog scans for.

      WithCulture is off, and it has to be. `name.<something>.ext` is how MSBuild spells a
      culture-specific resource, so AssignCulture reads the `en` in `core.en.json` as a
      culture and moves the file into a satellite assembly - `en/tabbit.resources.dll` -
      where GetManifestResourceNames on this assembly cannot see it. It builds and it runs;
      the catalog is simply empty. Which is the build doing the localization itself, in the
      one way this design ruled out: a language axis nobody can open a file to read.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Messages\Catalog\*.json"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Messages.%(Filename)%(Extension)"
                      WithCulture="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Code generation templates, embedded for the same reason as the readers: what ships
      cannot drift from what is committed, and a target cannot be pointed at a template of
      a different vintage.

      One file per generated language, flat, so a resource name has no directory
      separator in it.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="templates\*.sbn"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Templates.%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What a validation rule is compiled against, carried inside the tool.

    Rule compilation needs the metadata of every assembly a rule may name, and until now it read
    those off disk from the running process's own reference list. That works from a framework
    dependent build and not from a self-contained single-file one, where the assemblies live
    inside the executable and have no path to open - so validation refused to run there at all.

    Three things go in, and only three: the framework's reference assemblies, the contract, and
    Newtonsoft - which is in the contract's surface because `Json` answers with a `JToken`. What
    a rule may use is exactly this list, which is the same narrowing the contract assembly is
    for. The tool's own dependencies - Roslyn, Serilog, the database drivers - are deliberately
    not here.

    Reference assemblies are metadata with no code, so they compress to a fraction of their size;
    zipping them keeps this assembly from growing by the eleven megabytes they occupy on disk.

    spec/validation/validation-usability-and-assembly-output.md section 4.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      `BaseIntermediateOutputPath` rather than `IntermediateOutputPath`, and that is not a
      preference: this group is evaluated before the SDK has worked the second one out, so it
      would be empty here and these would land in the source tree. They did, and 170 reference
      assemblies sat beside the code waiting for somebody to `git add .`.
    -->
    <RuleReferenceStaging>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)rule-references\</RuleReferenceStaging>
    <RuleReferenceArchive>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)rule-references.zip</RuleReferenceArchive>

    <!--
      The second set: what generated C# is compiled against when a recipe asks for an assembly
      instead of sources. netstandard2.1 rather than this tool's own framework, because the
      consumer is somebody else's project - a game on Unity 6.5, a server on .NET 10 - and
      netstandard2.1 is the surface both accept.

      Two places are looked at, because the SDK puts them in different ones. The Windows
      installer lays the targeting pack down under `packs`, and a build there finds it with no
      network at all - which is what this used to assume for every platform. It is not true off
      Windows: the Linux and macOS SDKs ship no netstandard2.1 pack and leave it to NuGet, so
      that assumption failed the build on the first line of the first target that needed it.

      The `PackageDownload` below is what makes the second place a fact rather than a hope. It
      puts the package in the global cache and adds nothing to this project's own references,
      so the tool still compiles against what it compiled against before.
    -->
    <NetStandardRefPackDir>$(NetCoreTargetingPackRoot)/NETStandard.Library.Ref/2.1.0/ref/netstandard2.1</NetStandardRefPackDir>
    <NetStandardRefCacheDir>$(NuGetPackageRoot)netstandard.library.ref/2.1.0/ref/netstandard2.1</NetStandardRefCacheDir>

    <NetStandardRefDir Condition="Exists('$(NetStandardRefPackDir)')">$(NetStandardRefPackDir)</NetStandardRefDir>
    <NetStandardRefDir Condition="'$(NetStandardRefDir)' == ''">$(NetStandardRefCacheDir)</NetStandardRefDir>

    <NetStandardReferenceArchive>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)netstandard-references.zip</NetStandardReferenceArchive>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Downloaded, not referenced: `PackageDownload` populates the global package cache and
      contributes nothing to the compile, which is the whole of what is wanted here - the
      directory has to exist so it can be zipped into this assembly.
    -->
    <PackageDownload Include="NETStandard.Library.Ref" Version="[2.1.0]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Declared here rather than inside the target that fills it: resources are gathered before
      a target that could add one has run, so an item added later is an item nothing compiles in.
      The file is written by `EmbedRuleCompilationReferences` below, which runs first.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="$(RuleReferenceArchive)"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.RuleReferences.zip" />

    <EmbeddedResource Include="$(NetStandardReferenceArchive)"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.NetStandardReferences.zip" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="EmbedRuleCompilationReferences"
          BeforeTargets="PrepareResources"
          DependsOnTargets="ResolveReferences">

    <ItemGroup>
      <!--
        The base framework, taken from the targeting pack the compiler itself used - and only
        that one. ASP.NET Core's pack is beside it and is referenced by this tool for the history
        server, but a validation rule has no business naming a web framework, and what a rule may
        use is the decision this list is.
      -->
      <RuleReference Include="@(ReferencePathWithRefAssemblies)"
                     Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Contains('Microsoft.NETCore.App.Ref'))" />

      <!--
        The contract and the one package its surface names, taken from the built assemblies rather
        than their reference-only counterparts: the contract is written into a project's validation
        folder as a file, and the summaries an editor shows sit beside the real one.
      -->
      <RuleReference Include="@(ReferencePath)"
                     Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'Tabbit.Validation' or '%(Filename)' == 'Newtonsoft.Json'" />

      <RuleReference Include="@(ReferencePath->'%(RootDir)%(Directory)%(Filename).xml')"
                     Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'Tabbit.Validation'" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <RemoveDir Directories="$(RuleReferenceStaging)" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(RuleReference)" DestinationFolder="$(RuleReferenceStaging)" />

    <ZipDirectory SourceDirectory="$(RuleReferenceStaging)"
                  DestinationFile="$(RuleReferenceArchive)"
                  Overwrite="true" />

    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(NetStandardRefDir)')"
           Text="No netstandard2.1 reference assemblies were found, so the tool cannot be built with the references its assembly output compiles against. Looked in the SDK's targeting packs (`$(NetStandardRefPackDir)`) and then in the package cache (`$(NetStandardRefCacheDir)`); a restore populates the second one." />

    <ZipDirectory SourceDirectory="$(NetStandardRefDir)"
                  DestinationFile="$(NetStandardReferenceArchive)"
                  Overwrite="true" />
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The history page. Embedded for the same reason as everything else here, plus one of
      its own: the tool is expected to run on closed networks, so a page that fetches a
      stylesheet or a charting library from anywhere is a page that does not load.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="History\web\*"
                      LogicalName="Tabbit.Web.%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The history's store is internal: it is infrastructure for the targets and the query
      layer in this assembly, not an API. The suite drives it directly all the same,
      because what has to be checked is how it behaves against a real MySQL - the
      migration, the upserts, the concurrent write - and reaching that only through a
      conversion would make each of those cases a workbook.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Tabbit.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The serve mode puts the history behind an HTTP API and a page. Kestrel rather than
      HttpListener: on Windows, binding HttpListener to anything but localhost needs a URL
      reservation or an elevated process, and a team tool that has to run as administrator
      to be reachable is a team tool nobody reaches.

      A framework reference rather than a package, so nothing new is restored. It does mean
      a framework-dependent deployment now needs the ASP.NET Core runtime rather than the
      base one; publish self-contained where that is not wanted.
    -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The contract is a project of its own that happens to sit under this folder, so the SDK's
      globs would otherwise compile its sources - and its `obj` - into this assembly as well.
    -->
    <Compile Remove="Contract\**" />
    <EmbeddedResource Remove="Contract\**" />
    <None Remove="Contract\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      What a validation rule is compiled against. Its own assembly rather than part of this one,
      for two reasons that arrive together: the surface a rule author sees becomes a declaration
      rather than "whatever this assembly happens to make public", and rule compilation gets
      something the tool can carry - a single-file publish has no path to read an assembly from,
      and this one cannot embed a reference to itself.
      spec/validation/validation-usability-and-assembly-output.md section 5a.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="Contract\Tabbit.Validation.Contract.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="CommandLineParser" Version="2.9.1" />
    <!--
      Roslyn, for compiling the validation rules. The scripting layer on top of this is
      deliberately not referenced: what it adds - #r, #load, a globals object - is what a
      host makes unnecessary, since references arrive as MetadataReferences and shared code
      arrives as another syntax tree. Pinned to the version this SDK's own compiler is, so
      a rule file accepts exactly the C# the rest of the repository does.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="5.9.0" />
    <!--
      Scriban rather than Handlebars or Fluid: no dependencies of its own, and its `{{ }}`
      syntax does not collide with anything in the languages being generated - which
      matters, because a template for C++ is full of braces.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Scriban" Version="7.2.6" />
    <PackageReference Include="Google.Apis.Sheets.v4" Version="1.75.0.4178" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
    <!--
      The workbook reader. Streams a sheet a row at a time rather than inflating the
      workbook into an object model, which is what one 61 MiB workbook of 4.9 million cells
      costing 6,982 MB and 27.8 seconds bought us before.

      Pinned exactly, and the version matters more than it would for a settled library:
      this one is pre-1.0. What makes that acceptable is that the reader sits behind one
      file and the value gate in `XlsxReaderGateTests` can be re-run against any
      replacement - ExcelDataReader already passes it. Grounds, measurements and the
      rejected candidates are in `spec/import/streaming-workbook-reader.md`.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Sylvan.Data.Excel" Version="0.5.8" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog" Version="4.4.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.Console" Version="6.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="7.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Database export targets. MySqlConnector rather than MySql.Data: it is fully
         async and MIT licensed. -->
    <PackageReference Include="MySqlConnector" Version="2.6.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="MongoDB.Driver" Version="3.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="StackExchange.Redis" Version="3.1.31" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Native assets arrive with their own debug files, and those are neither reference-
    related nor ours - AllowedReferenceRelatedFileExtensions does not reach them.

    The one that mattered was SkiaSharp's 83 MB file, which arrived through NPOI, and
    neither package is a dependency any more. Kept for the same reason as the setting
    above: a debug file nobody asked for should not reach a publish quietly.

    Safe to drop all of them: this project's own debug information is embedded in the
    assembly.
  -->
  <Target Name="DropNativeDebugFiles" AfterTargets="ComputeResolvedFilesToPublishList">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)" Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.pdb'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
